use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior KeyboardShortcuts:
    description:
        Keyboard shortcuts are key combinations that trigger an action from
        anywhere in an app, or anywhere in one part of it, such as Ctrl+S to
        save or "/" to jump to search. They belong to the app, or to the
        component that owns the part where they work.

    combinations:
        A shortcut is one key, with or without modifier keys held down, such
        as Ctrl, Alt, Shift, or the command key. It can also be a short series
        of keys pressed one after another, such as G then I, which must be
        finished within a moment of starting.

    platforms:
        A shortcut can use the platform's main modifier, which is Command on
        Apple devices and Ctrl elsewhere, so one shortcut suits everyone.
        Shortcuts are shown to the user in their platform's own way, such as
        ⌘K on a Mac and Ctrl+K elsewhere.

    typing:
        Shortcuts never get in the way of typing. While the user is typing in
        a text box, a single key without modifiers, like "/", types as normal.
        Shortcuts that use Ctrl, Alt or Command still work there, unless the
        app says otherwise.

    scope:
        A shortcut can work across the whole app, or only while focus is in
        one part of it, such as an editor or a side panel. Where the same keys
        mean something in that part and in the whole app, the part wins while
        focus is in it. While a blocking panel, such as a dialog, is open,
        shortcuts for the page behind it don't work.

    conflicts:
        When two shortcuts claim the same keys in the same place, the more
        specific one wins: a series of keys over a single key, and more
        modifiers over fewer. Whether clashes are reported, and what happens
        when neither is more specific, is up to the chosen library.

    holding:
        Whether holding the keys down repeats the action is up to the app.

    platformKeys:
        Shortcuts don't take over keys that the platform or assistive tools
        rely on, such as copy and paste, or a screen reader's own commands.

    keyboardLayouts:
        Shortcuts work on keyboards other than US English. Letters and digits
        with modifiers work everywhere, but symbols move between layouts, so
        shortcuts that use them are chosen with care. Keys that start an
        accented letter don't trigger anything.

    listing:
        Each shortcut can carry a short description, so the app can list them,
        such as on a help screen or beside a menu item. Whether the user can
        change a shortcut by pressing the keys they want is up to the chosen
        library.

    nonGoals:
        - Not for keys inside a component, such as the arrow keys in a list.
            Those are Traversal and the component's own keyboard rules
        - Not a command palette. A searchable list of actions is a separate
            component that can show their shortcuts
